Myth: Long ago, when rivers first learned to flow, there lived a water spirit named Kora who dwelled in the deepest pool of the oldest stream. She possessed a face of such radiant beauty that it could calm storms and make flowers bloom, but she had never seen it herself, for water cannot look upon itself.

One day, a proud peacock came to drink, declaring his reflection the most magnificent sight in all the world. Kora, curious, rose to the surface and asked, "What does it mean to see oneself?"

The peacock, glimpsing her face in the water, fell silent with wonder. "Your beauty surpasses mine a thousand times," he whispered. "Yet you cannot witness it?"

"I have no way to look upon myself," Kora said sadly. "My gift exists only for others."

The peacock, humbled and moved, made an offering. "I will tell every creature of your beauty if you grant all water the gift you possess."

Kora agreed, and touched the surface of her pool. From that moment, all still water became a mirror, reflecting back whatever gazed upon it. The peacock spread word of her magic far and wide.

And that is why every stream and lake now offers its gift—so that all beings, even water itself, might see their own face and never feel unseen.
