One day, a vain songbird flew to the mountains, boasting that his voice was the finest ever heard. Kora, curious, emerged from her cave and sang a single note. The bird fell silent, ashamed, for her voice held the music of wind, water, and starlight combined.

"Please," the bird begged, "teach me to sing like you."

But Kora shook her head sadly. "My gift cannot be given, only shared. Yet I have no one to share it with."

The bird, moved by her loneliness, made a bargain. "If you release your voice into the mountains, I will carry the news of your beauty to all the world."

Kora sang out across the valleys, and the mountains, touched by her loneliness, caught her voice and sent it back to her—the first echo. From that day forward, anyone who calls into the mountains receives an answer, so that no voice need ever be alone again.

The songbird kept his promise, and that is why we still seek the mountains when we wish to be heard.

